        "result": "The revised recommendation is sound in direction. Its weakness is that it still lets the engine comparison decide too much before the KXM-owned contracts are written down.\n\n- **Restart semantics are the sharpest risk and get one line.** OpenCode restart is at-least-once, assumes the prior owner is dead, and can repeat external effects. That is not a host detail. It means the Runtime effect ledger must be authoritative over the engine's own recovery, and the engine must never be allowed to resume a job the Runtime has not reconciled. Say that explicitly as a contract, not as a caveat.\n\n- **First-admission-wins on session/type is the same problem from the other side.** The plan correctly says KXM needs its own strict command hash. It should also say that hash must be computed before the durable inbox sees the input, otherwise the inbox admits a duplicate the Runtime later rejects and the two stores disagree.\n\n- **Unbounded live pubsub deserves an M2 acceptance criterion, not a mention.** Paged replay-to-live with a Synced marker is good. The bounded-streams milestone should state what happens on backpressure: drop, block, or spill to the durable log.\n\n- **ToolCalled before execution fibers is an ordering hazard for evidence.** If the Runtime records evidence from bus events, it can record a tool call that never ran. Evidence should key off the started event from the durable execution claim, not off ToolCalled.\n\n- **The subagent distinction is right but the milestone that resolves it is missing.** One-shot versus continuable children and a runner without checkpoints or token budget are named as open, then M6 lists typed graph and budget without saying which engine surface backs continuation. That is the place where the comparison result actually lands.\n\n- **Fixture comparison in M9 is too late.** If the engine choice is deferred pending comparison, the fixture harness belongs before M6, because the graph and budget design will be shaped by whichever session model wins.\n\nWhat would move me toward OpenCode as primary base: its durable job recovery and execution claim survive a real crash test under the Runtime's reconciliation, and its restart can be gated so the Runtime decides resumption. What would move me toward DSH: the tool pool barrier proves enforceable across process restart, which neither Pi nor OpenCode currently offers.\n\nStrongest missing contract: a written ownership rule for external effects across crash. Who may retry, on what proof, and which store is the tiebreaker when engine state and Runtime ledger disagree.",
